      "text": "To find the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees, we start with the given temperature formula:\n\n\\[ T(t) = -t^2 + 12t + 50 \\]\n\nWe know that the temperature was 77 degrees, so we set \\( T(t) = 77 \\) and solve for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77 \\]\n\nFirst, we move all terms to one side of the equation to set it to zero:\n\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0 \\]\n\\[ -t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNext, we multiply the entire equation by -1 to make the leading coefficient positive:\n\n\\[ t^2 - 12t + 27 = 0 \\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula, which is given by:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\]\n\nFor our equation \\( t^2 - 12t + 27 = 0 \\), the coefficients are \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\). Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2} \\]\n\nThis gives us two potential solutions for \\( t \\):\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9 \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\]\n\nThe largest \\( t \\) value is 9. Therefore, the largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]",
